```java
class Shape {
    public double calculateArea() {
        return 0.0; // Default or generic implementation
    }
}

class Circle extends Shape {
    private double radius;

    public Circle(double radius) {
        this.radius = radius;
    }

    @Override // Good practice to use @Override annotation
    public double calculateArea() {
        return Math.PI * radius * radius; // Specific implementation for Circle
    }
}
```
Here, `Circle` overrides `calculateArea()` to provide its specific area calculation, demonstrating polymorphism.

The `super` keyword is used within an overriding method in a subclass to explicitly call the overridden method's implementation from the superclass. This is useful when the subclass wants to extend or augment the superclass's behavior rather than completely replace it, or to reuse common logic from the parent.
Example:
```java
class Parent { void show() { System.out.println("Parent's show()"); } }
class Child extends Parent {
    @Override
    void show() {
        super.show(); // Calls Parent's show()
        System.out.println("Child's show()");
    }
}
```
